FEX: SMC tracking of shared memory with an executable view, on 16 KiB host pages (SteamARM)

SMC tracking (mtrack) write-protects every writable view of shared memory
whose code FEX translated through another view (MarkGuestExecutableRange).
Two holes, both hit by x86-64 ART's JIT code cache, a memfd mapped as an
executable view and a writable view:

1. A guest mprotect that makes such a writable view writable again lifted
   that protection on the host, and FEX only invalidated translations in the
   mprotected range itself, where there are none. ART's ScopedCodeCacheWrite
   flips the writable view read-write around every code write, so rewrites
   of freed code went unseen. Now the host pages of that view which alias an
   executable view stay read-only (the guest sees them writable); each first
   write faults once and is handled below.
2. The fault handler's shared-memory branch flushed and unprotected one
   4 KiB page of each mirror, while under the runtime the SMC page of a
   64-bit guest is the 16 KiB host page (fex-lxrt-guest-base.patch):
   unprotecting 4 KiB makes the whole host page writable, and writes to the
   other three guest pages of it went unseen. It now flushes and unprotects
   the same SMC page as the non-shared branch, clipped to the mapping, in
   every mirror.

MEASURED (SteamARM benchmarks/stage25-art-x86-fex.txt): after code cache
collections ART ran old methods' code and computed wrong results, 3 runs of
3; correct with FEX_SMCCHECKS=full. tests/android/x86_dualview.c, stale
results of 2304: 89 before (2.), 2112 with "toggle" (1. and 2.), 0 after;
with 1. alone, 89. Invalidating every mirror's
translations at each mprotect instead (a first version of this patch) was
correct but made a JIT-heavy run 4.4 times slower. The first write after the
host mprotect(RW) and before the re-protection is the one window left; ART
writes its code cache from one thread under a lock.

diff --git a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SyscallsSMCTracking.cpp b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SyscallsSMCTracking.cpp
--- a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SyscallsSMCTracking.cpp
+++ b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SyscallsSMCTracking.cpp
@@ -118,20 +118,35 @@
     if (Entry->second.Flags.Shared) {
       LOGMAN_THROW_A_FMT(Entry->second.Resource, "VMA tracking error");
 
-      auto Offset = FaultBase - Entry->first + Entry->second.Offset;
+      // The whole SMC page (a 16 KiB host page under the runtime, for 64-bit
+      // guests), clipped to this mapping: MarkGuestExecutableRange protected
+      // the writable mirrors that way, and unprotecting one 4 KiB page of a
+      // host page makes the whole host page writable. Flushing only the
+      // faulting 4 KiB of the mirrors let later writes to the rest of that
+      // host page go unseen: ART's JIT code cache (a memfd mapped twice, an
+      // executable view and a writable one) reused freed code and FEX ran
+      // stale translations of the old methods (MEASURED: wrong results after
+      // code cache collections, SteamARM benchmarks/stage25-art-x86-fex.txt).
+      const auto SpanBase = std::max<uint64_t>(FEXCore::AlignDown(FaultAddress, SMCPage), Entry->first);
+      const auto SpanTop = std::min<uint64_t>(FEXCore::AlignDown(FaultAddress, SMCPage) + SMCPage, Entry->first + Entry->second.Length);
+      const auto OffsetBase = SpanBase - Entry->first + Entry->second.Offset;
+      const auto OffsetTop = SpanTop - Entry->first + Entry->second.Offset;
 
       auto VMA = Entry->second.Resource->FirstVMA;
       LOGMAN_THROW_A_FMT(VMA, "VMA tracking error");
 
-      // Flush all mirrors, remap the page writable as needed
+      // Flush all mirrors, remap the pages writable as needed
       do {
-        if (VMA->Offset <= Offset && (VMA->Offset + VMA->Length) > Offset) {
-          auto FaultBaseMirrored = Offset - VMA->Offset + VMA->Base;
+        const auto MirrorOffsetBase = std::max<uint64_t>(VMA->Offset, OffsetBase);
+        const auto MirrorOffsetTop = std::min<uint64_t>(VMA->Offset + VMA->Length, OffsetTop);
+        if (MirrorOffsetBase < MirrorOffsetTop) {
+          auto FaultBaseMirrored = MirrorOffsetBase - VMA->Offset + VMA->Base;
+          const auto MirrorLength = MirrorOffsetTop - MirrorOffsetBase;
 
           if (VMA->Prot.Writable) {
-            _SyscallHandler->TM.InvalidateGuestCodeRange(Thread, FaultBaseMirrored, FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SIZE, UnprotectRegionCallback);
+            _SyscallHandler->TM.InvalidateGuestCodeRange(Thread, FaultBaseMirrored, MirrorLength, UnprotectRegionCallback);
           } else {
-            _SyscallHandler->TM.InvalidateGuestCodeRange(Thread, FaultBaseMirrored, FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SIZE);
+            _SyscallHandler->TM.InvalidateGuestCodeRange(Thread, FaultBaseMirrored, MirrorLength);
           }
         }
       } while ((VMA = VMA->ResourceNextVMA));
@@ -559,6 +574,55 @@
     }
 
     TrackMprotect(Thread, addr, len, prot);
+
+    // A view of shared memory made writable while another view of the same
+    // memory is executable: the host mprotect just lifted the write
+    // protection SMC tracking keeps on every writable mirror of translated
+    // code (MarkGuestExecutableRange), so rewrites through this view would go
+    // unseen. Keep the host pages read-only instead; the guest sees them
+    // writable (VMA tracking), and each first write faults once, is flushed
+    // from the mirrors and unprotected by HandleSegfault, one SMC page at a
+    // time. ART's JIT code cache is such a pair (a memfd mapped as an
+    // executable view and a writable view) and ScopedCodeCacheWrite flips the
+    // writable view read-write around every code write: without this x86-64
+    // ART ran stale translations after code cache collections (MEASURED,
+    // SteamARM benchmarks/stage25-art-x86-fex.txt).
+    if ((prot & PROT_WRITE) && SMCChecks == FEXCore::Config::CONFIG_SMC_MTRACK) {
+      const auto Base = reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(addr);
+      const auto Top = Base + FEXCore::AlignUp(len, FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SIZE);
+      const auto SMCPage = SMCPageSize();
+      auto Mapping = VMATracking.VMAs.lower_bound(Top);
+      while (Mapping != VMATracking.VMAs.begin()) {
+        --Mapping;
+        const auto MapBase = Mapping->first;
+        const auto MapTop = MapBase + Mapping->second.Length;
+        if (MapTop <= Base) {
+          break;
+        }
+        if (!Mapping->second.Flags.Shared || !Mapping->second.Resource) {
+          continue;
+        }
+        const auto Lo = std::max(MapBase, Base);
+        const auto Hi = std::min(MapTop, Top);
+        const auto OffsetLo = Lo - MapBase + Mapping->second.Offset;
+        const auto OffsetHi = Hi - MapBase + Mapping->second.Offset;
+        for (auto VMA = Mapping->second.Resource->FirstVMA; VMA; VMA = VMA->ResourceNextVMA) {
+          const auto MirrorLo = std::max<uint64_t>(VMA->Offset, OffsetLo);
+          const auto MirrorHi = std::min<uint64_t>(VMA->Offset + VMA->Length, OffsetHi);
+          if (MirrorLo >= MirrorHi || VMA->Base == MapBase || !VMA->Prot.Executable) {
+            continue;
+          }
+          // This view's pages that alias executable memory, whole SMC pages,
+          // clipped to the range the guest mprotected.
+          const auto ProtLo = std::max(Lo, FEXCore::AlignDown(MirrorLo - OffsetLo + Lo, SMCPage));
+          const auto ProtHi = std::min(Hi, FEXCore::AlignUp(MirrorHi - OffsetLo + Lo, SMCPage));
+          if (ProtLo < ProtHi) {
+            auto rv = ::mprotect(FEX::HLE::G2H(reinterpret_cast<void*>(ProtLo)), ProtHi - ProtLo, PROT_READ);
+            LogMan::Throw::AFmt(rv == 0, "mprotect({}, {}) failed", ProtLo, ProtHi - ProtLo);
+          }
+        }
+      }
+    }
   }
 
   InvalidateCodeRangeIfNecessary(Thread, reinterpret_cast<uint64_t>(addr), len, false);
